<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yingya Shenglan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1451">1451</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as The Overall Survey of the Ocean's Shores</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yingya Shenglan, The Overall Survey of the Ocean's Shores, is a book by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a> about the countries visited on the Ming treasure voyages led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a>, published in 1451. Ma Huan served as an interpreter on the fourth, sixth and seventh voyages and wrote it with Guo Chongli, who sailed on three of them, from the notes they had kept; he began arranging them after his return in 1415 and wrote a foreword in 1416. He wrote in a colloquial style, which Zhang Sheng later rewrote in a literary one. No copy of the original edition survives: the text is known from later versions that differ from one another, among them those in the Guochao Diangu and the Jilu Huibian of about 1617.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yingya-shenglan.jpg" alt="Yingya Shenglan" width="330" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ma Huan (馬歡) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yingya_Shenglan_(Jilu_Huiban),_first_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>To the foreword he wrote in 1416, together with a poem, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a> later added the posthumous title of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>, which was conferred in 1424. A second foreword was written by Ma Jing in 1444. The afterword of 1451 came from the imperial clerk Gu Po, whom Guo Chongli, helped by his friend Lu Tingyong, persuaded to write it, and the book was published the same year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The version in the Guochao Diangu was edited by Zhu Dangmian at an unknown date between 1451 and 1644 and fills 42 folios in chapter 106 of that work. The National Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> holds this edition, possibly the only surviving example; it has no foreword, poem or afterword, and nothing is known of the documents on which it was based. A copy at the Columbia University Libraries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> shows that its copyist drew on other texts, since it adds the forewords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a> and Ma Jing, the poem, corrected readings and a few words.</p><p>The version in the Jilu Huibian, published by Shen Jiefu and Chen Yuting, fills 47 folios in chapter 62 and includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a>'s foreword, his poem and Gu Po's afterword. Copies are held at Cambridge University Library, the Institut des Hautes Études Chinoises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the Library of Congress, Harvard Library and the National Palace Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the Sinological Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>.</p><p>Zhang Sheng's condensed rewriting appeared in a posthumous edition of his works published by his son in 1522, and in 22 folios of chapter 63 of the Jilu Huibian. A further version, based on an unknown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, fills 48 folios in the first chapter of the Shengchao Yishi, published by Wu Miguang in 1824.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yingya Shenglan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
